{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-44059","model_call":17,"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"death valley national park\" \"climate change\" \"warmer\" \"drier\")"},"record":{"query":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"death valley national park\" \"climate change\" \"warmer\" \"drier\")"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_48_1050970337#3_1900125130","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q1565","rank":1,"snippet":"Study to look at effects of climate change on wildlife Contact the Park Tools \" , \"segment\": \"Species that lived at low elevations expanded their range upwards. Animals living at higher elevations suffered severe reduction in their range with no place upwards to go. Death Valley holds the world high temperature record: 134°F (56.6°C). The vulnerability of desert parks to <cover>climate change makes this work especially important. The National Park Service will be able to use the data from this study to identify wildlife species that might be especially vulnerable to changes in climate. Josh Hoines, Chief of Natural and Cultural Resource Management in Death Valley National Park, said, \"Predicted changes in climate across the desert southwest of a warmer and drier </cover>future make Death Valley and the park's extreme climate an ideal place to study how species may respond to those changes. For many species, Death Valley may already represent their physiological limits. This study will shed insights as to how species have responded to climate shifts over the last hundred years and help managers to protect species and their habitats for the next 100 years.\" For more information, ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_48_1050970337#3_1900125130","rank":1,"relevance":2}]},"time":191},"model_calls":17,"document_judgments":6,"relevance_3":0,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
