{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-44059","model_call":56,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1598"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q1598"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_44_1318144478#10_2773776401","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1598","rank":3,"snippet":"you can make time to see the amazing Charcoal Kilns. Death Valley in Four or More Days With four or more days in Death Valley National Park, there’s little need to rush. See the major sights and spend a day in the high country. Go for short and long walks. Watch the rising sun cast a glow on the <cover>badlands at Zabriskie Point. See Cathedral Rock in Golden Canyon shine a crimson red in late afternoon light. And perhaps watch a full moon illuminate the Mesquite Flat Sand Dunes. You might even get in a round at the world’s lowest golf course, and have time to relax at your campsite or around the swimming pool at Furnace Creek Ranch or Inn. You can take the drive over Towne Pass to the west and see Panamint Dry Lake </cover>and a stellar view from Father Crowley Viewpoint. With this much time, you can do some of the park’s stellar day hikes, like out to Darwin Falls—yes, a 25-foot waterfall that tumbles into a cool, clear pool in the middle of the park.\" , \"start_char\": 5291.000000 , \"title\"","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_19_690527615#3_771752803","rank":1,"relevance":3},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_19_690527615#2_771751099","rank":2,"relevance":3}]},"time":8},"model_calls":56,"document_judgments":20,"relevance_3":3,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
