{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-44059","model_call":81,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1616"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q1616"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_40_1259303777#8_2693500043","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1616","rank":4,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_40_1259303777#8_2693500043\" , \"end_char\": 5353.000000 , \"headings\": \"About Minerals & Crystals About Minerals & Crystals What Are Crystals? Minerals, Crystals or Rocks? How Do Inorganic Crystals Form? Mineral Classes Economics of Crystals and Minerals Terminology and Illustrations RELATED ARTICLES \" , \"segment\": \"This is a sedimentary rock formation that's part of <cover>Zabriskie Point, located within Death Valley, California. It's composed of sediments from Furnace Creek Lake </cover>which dried up roughly 5 million years ago. Millions of years of erosion have left the sediment layers exposed. Metamorphic Rock - Rock that occurred when existing sedimentary or igneous rock was exposed to pressures and in some cases temperature changes that altered their original mineralogy. Metamorphic rock known as \"granite gneiss\". Formed from metamorphosed granite. How Do Inorganic Crystals Form? Crystals can form from a variety of different processes, including: Evaporite Deposits - These are mineral formations that occur as a result of processes at the earth’s surface. Crystals form from solutions containing minerals that become concentrated by dehydration/evaporation of an aqueous solution.\" ,","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_40_1259303777#8_2693500043","rank":4,"relevance":2}]},"time":4},"model_calls":81,"document_judgments":31,"relevance_3":4,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
{"actor":"server","event":"request","topic_id":"2024-44059","model_call":81,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1616"},"model_calls":81,"document_judgments":31,"relevance_3":4,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-44059","model_call":81,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1616"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q1616"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_01_1809342308#3_2668230960","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1616","rank":5,"snippet":"3_2668230960\" , \"end_char\": 2902.000000 , \"headings\": \"Interesting facts about Death Valley Interesting facts about Death Valley\" , \"segment\": \"Over 80 species of birds have been spotted around the pond. Artist’s Palette is an area on the face of the Black Mountains noted for a variety of rock colors. These colors are caused by the oxidation of different metals. It is the most colorful area in Death Valley. <cover>Zabriskie Point is a part of the Amargosa Range located east of Death Valley, noted for its erosional landscape. It is composed of sediments from Furnace Creek Lake, </cover>which dried up 5 million years ago—long before Death Valley came into existence. The geologic history of Death Valley is extremely complex and involves different types of fault activity at various periods, in addition to crustal sinking and even some volcanic activity. Rock art and artifacts indicate a human presence dating back at least 9,000 years. Death Valley is home to the Timbisha tribe of Native Americans, formerly known as the Panamint Shoshone, who have inhabited the valley for at least the past millennium. ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_01_1809342308#3_2668230960","rank":5,"relevance":2}]},"time":7},"model_calls":81,"document_judgments":31,"relevance_3":4,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
