{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-44059","model_call":85,"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"zabriskie point\" erosion \"furnace creek lake\")"},"record":{"query":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"zabriskie point\" erosion \"furnace creek lake\")"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_40_1259303777#7_2693498447","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q1620","rank":1,"snippet":"changes quickly. In this instance, large crystals were allowed to form with slow cooling, only to be interrupted by a sudden decreasing temperature change that sped up the crystallization process, resulting in smaller crystals. Sedimentary Rock - Rock that formed as a result of eroded materials from prior formed rocks being deposited along ocean floors, river beds, lake beds, etc., along with deposition of minerals from the water. Over years these depositions are compacted by natural forces, later to solidify into a solid mass (rock). This is a sedimentary rock formation that's part of <cover>Zabriskie Point, located within Death Valley, California. It's composed of sediments from Furnace Creek Lake which dried up roughly 5 million years ago. Millions of years of erosion </cover>have left the sediment layers exposed. Metamorphic Rock - Rock that occurred when existing sedimentary or igneous rock was exposed to pressures and in some cases temperature changes that altered their original mineralogy. Metamorphic rock known as \"granite gneiss\".\" , \"start_char\": 3874.000000 , \"title\": \"About Minerals & Crystals - FossilEra.com\" , \"url\": \"https://www.fossilera.com/pages/about-minerals-crystals\" }","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_40_1259303777#7_2693498447","rank":1,"relevance":2}]},"time":62},"model_calls":85,"document_judgments":32,"relevance_3":4,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
