{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-44059","model_call":85,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1620"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q1620"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_40_1259303777#8_2693500043","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1620","rank":2,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_40_1259303777#8_2693500043\" , \"end_char\": 5353.000000 , \"headings\": \"About Minerals & Crystals About Minerals & Crystals What Are Crystals? Minerals, Crystals or Rocks? How Do Inorganic Crystals Form? Mineral Classes Economics of Crystals and Minerals Terminology and Illustrations RELATED ARTICLES \" , \"segment\": \"This is a sedimentary rock formation that's part of <cover>Zabriskie Point, located within Death Valley, California. It's composed of sediments from Furnace Creek Lake which dried up roughly 5 million years ago. Millions of years of erosion </cover>have left the sediment layers exposed. Metamorphic Rock - Rock that occurred when existing sedimentary or igneous rock was exposed to pressures and in some cases temperature changes that altered their original mineralogy. Metamorphic rock known as \"granite gneiss\". Formed from metamorphosed granite. How Do Inorganic Crystals Form? Crystals can form from a variety of different processes, including: Evaporite Deposits - These are mineral formations that occur as a result of processes at the earth’s surface. Crystals form from solutions containing minerals that become concentrated by dehydration/evaporation of an aqueous solution.\" ,","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_40_1259303777#8_2693500043","rank":2,"relevance":2}]},"time":6},"model_calls":85,"document_judgments":32,"relevance_3":4,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
{"actor":"server","event":"request","topic_id":"2024-44059","model_call":85,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1620"},"model_calls":85,"document_judgments":32,"relevance_3":4,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-44059","model_call":85,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1620"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q1620"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_19_690527615#2_771751099","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1620","rank":3,"snippet":"along <cover>Furnace Creek Lake was dry, but not nearly as dry as in the present. Camels, mastodons, horses, carnivores, and birds left tracks in the lakeshore muds, along with fossilized grass and reeds. Borates, which made up a large portion of Death Valley's historical past were concentrated in the lakebeds from hot spring waters and alteration of rhyolite in the nearby volcanic field. Weathering and alteration by thermal waters are also responsible for the variety of colors represented there. Close-up of Red Cathedral, to the right of Manly Beacon Regional mountains building to the west influenced the climate to become more and more arid, causing the lake to dry up, and creating a dry lake. Subsequent widening and sinking of Death Valley and the additional uplift of today's Black Mountains tilted the area. This provided the necessary relief to accomplish the erosion that produced the badlands we see today. The dark-colored material capping the badland ridges (to the left in the panoramic photograph) is lava from eruptions that occurred three to five million years ago.\" , \"start_char\": 1482.000000 , \"title\": \"Zabriskie Point - </cover>Wikipedia\"","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_19_690527615#2_771751099","rank":3,"relevance":3}]},"time":9},"model_calls":85,"document_judgments":32,"relevance_3":4,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
